1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,550
Zip.

2
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,995
Two rows of tiny teeth,

3
00:00:01,745 --> 00:00:04,130
locked together in under a second.

4
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:06,534
It’s one of the most-used machines in your life,

5
00:00:06,284 --> 00:00:08,537
and you’ve probably never looked inside.

6
00:00:09,487 --> 00:00:11,036
This is an ordinary zip,

7
00:00:10,786 --> 00:00:12,227
on an ordinary jacket.

8
00:00:11,977 --> 00:00:13,272
Two fabric tapes,

9
00:00:13,022 --> 00:00:14,378
two rows of teeth,

10
00:00:14,128 --> 00:00:15,607
and a little slider.

11
00:00:15,357 --> 00:00:17,015
But look through the slider,

12
00:00:16,765 --> 00:00:19,277
and you’ll find a beautifully simple machine.

13
00:00:22,127 --> 00:00:23,071
Lift it apart,

14
00:00:22,821 --> 00:00:24,311
and there’s the pull tab,

15
00:00:24,061 --> 00:00:25,104
the slider body,

16
00:00:24,854 --> 00:00:26,195
the two rows of teeth,

17
00:00:25,945 --> 00:00:28,178
and the fabric tapes they’re clamped to,

18
00:00:27,928 --> 00:00:29,319
with stops at each end,

19
00:00:29,069 --> 00:00:30,707
so the slider can’t run off.

20
00:00:30,457 --> 00:00:32,943
Each tooth is clamped tightly onto a corded

21
00:00:32,693 --> 00:00:33,826
edge of the tape,

22
00:00:33,576 --> 00:00:35,178
so it can’t slip or twist.

23
00:00:36,828 --> 00:00:38,838
Look closely at a single tooth.

24
00:00:38,588 --> 00:00:40,444
Each one has a little bump on top,

25
00:00:40,194 --> 00:00:41,578
and a hollow underneath.

26
00:00:41,328 --> 00:00:43,167
And the teeth on each side are

27
00:00:42,917 --> 00:00:45,127
staggered, so every tooth faces a gap

28
00:00:44,877 --> 00:00:46,187
on the opposite row.

29
00:00:47,636 --> 00:00:49,506
The secret is inside the slider.

30
00:00:49,256 --> 00:00:51,286
Its channel is shaped like a Y.

31
00:00:51,036 --> 00:00:51,806
Pull it up,

32
00:00:51,556 --> 00:00:53,839
and the two rows are guided in at an angle,

33
00:00:53,589 --> 00:00:54,926
then squeezed together.

34
00:00:54,676 --> 00:00:57,166
Each bump drops into the hollow of the tooth

35
00:00:56,916 --> 00:00:58,438
beside it, and they lock,

36
00:00:58,188 --> 00:00:59,354
one after another.

37
00:01:01,055 --> 00:01:02,487
Pull it the other way,

38
00:01:02,237 --> 00:01:04,047
and a little wedge at the top

39
00:01:03,797 --> 00:01:05,498
of the Y prises them apart,

40
00:01:05,248 --> 00:01:06,305
tooth by tooth.

41
00:01:06,055 --> 00:01:08,415
It’s the same simple machine as an axe

42
00:01:08,165 --> 00:01:10,026
blade, just very, very small.

43
00:01:11,726 --> 00:01:14,016
So why can’t you just pull a closed zip apart?

44
00:01:13,766 --> 00:01:16,171
Pull sideways, and every bump is trapped

45
00:01:15,921 --> 00:01:16,926
in its hollow.

46
00:01:16,676 --> 00:01:18,576
The teeth only let go when they’re

47
00:01:18,326 --> 00:01:20,275
bent apart at just the right angle,

48
00:01:20,025 --> 00:01:22,217
and that’s exactly what the slider does.

49
00:01:22,917 --> 00:01:24,487
Some sliders even lock.

50
00:01:24,237 --> 00:01:25,771
Lay the pull tab flat,

51
00:01:25,521 --> 00:01:28,047
and a tiny pin drops between the teeth,

52
00:01:27,797 --> 00:01:30,323
so the zip can’t creep open on its own.

53
00:01:31,023 --> 00:01:32,165
And on a jacket,

54
00:01:31,915 --> 00:01:34,116
the bottom end has a pin and a box,

55
00:01:33,866 --> 00:01:35,843
so the two sides can come apart

56
00:01:35,593 --> 00:01:38,797
completely, then slot back together for the next zip.

57
00:01:39,497 --> 00:01:43,117
It began with Whitcomb Judson’s clasp locker in 1893.

58
00:01:42,867 --> 00:01:45,715
In 1913, Gideon Sundbäck redesigned it,

59
00:01:45,465 --> 00:01:48,647
packing ten or eleven teeth into every inch.

60
00:01:48,397 --> 00:01:50,177
And in 1923, B.

61
00:01:49,927 --> 00:01:50,177
F.

62
00:01:49,927 --> 00:01:51,876
Goodrich put it on rubber boots,

63
00:01:51,626 --> 00:01:52,990
called it the zipper,

64
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:53,999
and the name stuck.

65
00:01:55,349 --> 00:01:56,799
Put it all back together.

66
00:01:56,549 --> 00:01:57,690
The slider guides,

67
00:01:57,440 --> 00:01:58,729
the channel squeezes,

68
00:01:58,479 --> 00:02:00,509
and every tooth locks into the next.

69
00:02:00,259 --> 00:02:01,975
Hundreds of tiny locks,

70
00:02:01,725 --> 00:02:03,505
closed in a single pull.

71
00:02:04,555 --> 00:02:06,685
If you enjoyed this, subscribe.

72
00:02:06,435 --> 00:02:09,545
Next time, we’ll open up another everyday machine.

